<p>An Iran-linked hacking group, Handala Hack Team, said on March 27 that it had broken into FBI Director Kash Patel&#x27;s personal Gmail account and posted what appeared to be authentic personal photos, a resume, and years-old correspondence online. A Justice Department official confirmed the breach and said the published material appeared authentic. The FBI said it had taken steps to reduce any risk and that the exposed information was old and did not include government data. The breach fits a broader pattern of cyber operations attributed to Handala, which Western researchers and US officials link to Iranian cyber units. Several reports said the group recently claimed responsibility for attacks involving medical technology company Stryker and data tied to Lockheed Martin employees, pointing to a wider campaign aimed at embarrassing US officials and pressuring American targets as the West Asia war continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qZkECQhGnoVnxz7mulIB420Mo_k9-Mh8CtFVjoNpw6aT3SQHpa5detqWLDIZjvcFTEyPf0raRfznlf3u59uNqL2pNgrwSrLbSFdQTORCClD-s8JYxs8p4Ksu80PitWSVl-3lNA4mhpwxJOnDqmJo1heol7sTaecNXbmFAvZAbE0KMONiX3nEZjasDNRvAvvYDa_Ej8lTkEaK1JHHyGYat39T7WPuzA2y9RgjAbQwfB-THxnTRUaM8j_Z5JpIo_tR4T8h1Y_EwqIbfA344w' alt='FILE PHOTO: FBI Director Kash Patel announces the apprehension of Ryan Wedding, a former Canadian Olympic snowboarder who was on the FBI&#x27;s Ten Most Wanted Fugitive list, during a press conference in Ontario, California, U.S., January 23, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Reward offered: The US State Department offered a reward of up to $10 million for information leading to the identification of Handala members, underscoring how seriously Washington is taking the group&#x27;s activity.</li><li>Email age: Files reviewed by reporters were mostly dated from 2010 to 2019, with Politico saying most emails ran from 2012 to 2014 and included at least one from 2022.</li><li>Previous exposure: Reuters reported that the Gmail address cited by Handala matched one previously linked to Patel in earlier data breaches preserved by dark-web intelligence firm District 4 Labs.</li><li>Broader claims: Handala also claimed this week that it had stolen the names and details of about two dozen Lockheed Martin employees after US officials linked the group to a March attack on Stryker that disrupted roughly 200,000 devices.</li></ul><blockquote>We are aware of malicious actors targeting Director Patel’s personal email information, and we have taken all necessary steps to mitigate potential risks associated with this activity. - FBI</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>FBI: The bureau said it was aware of the targeting, had taken steps to reduce risk, and believed the exposed material was old and did not include government information. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/27/fbi-kash-patel-email-hacks-handala-00848519'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Justice Department official: A Justice Department official confirmed that Patel&#x27;s personal email had been breached and said the material published online appeared authentic. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1985963/iran-linked-hackers-breach-fbi-director-kash-patels-personal-email-publish-excerpts-online'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Handala Hack Team: The group cast the breach as a symbolic humiliation, posting that Patel would now see his name added to its list of hacked victims. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3348214/pro-iran-hackers-say-they-breached-fbi-chief-patels-email-account-post-personal-files'>SCMP</a>)</li><li>Security researchers: Western researchers cited by Reuters say Handala is likely one of several personas used by Iranian government cyberintelligence units, rather than an independent activist group. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-iran-linked-hackers-breach-fbi-director-kash-patel-email/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/19